## 11. API DESIGN

Create clean, versionable APIs.

Potential endpoints:

POST /api/analyze

POST /api/reports

GET /api/reports

GET /api/alerts

GET /api/alerts/[id]

GET /api/dashboard

GET /api/moderation/reports

PATCH /api/moderation/reports/[id]

POST /api/auth/register

POST /api/auth/login

Use appropriate HTTP methods and status codes.

Validate all incoming requests.

Protect private endpoints.

Return consistent response formats.

Never expose stack traces or internal database errors to users.

## 12. TESTING REQUIREMENTS

Write tests for:

- Message analyzer rules.
- Risk score calculations.
- Input validation.
- Report submission.
- Public report visibility.
- Authorization.
- Moderator actions.
- API error handling.
- Database operations.

Add browser tests for:
- Landing page.
- Message analysis.
- Registration and login.
- Report submission.
- Alert browsing.
- Moderation workflow.

Run:

npm run lint
npx tsc --noEmit
npx prisma validate
npm run test

If a script is missing, create it and configure the relevant testing framework.

Fix errors instead of simply reporting them.

## 13. BUSINESS MODEL

Prepare the architecture for a potential subscription business.

Possible future plans:

FREE:
- Basic message analysis.
- Educational resources.
- Limited community alerts.

PERSONAL PREMIUM:
- Expanded alerts.
- Personalized safety monitoring.
- Saved analysis history, with explicit consent.

BUSINESS:
- Business dashboard.
- Organization alerts.
- Monitoring and analytics.
- Team access.

ENTERPRISE:
- API access.
- Integrations.
- Advanced reporting.
- Custom security workflows.

Do not implement fake payment processing.

If billing is implemented, use a legitimate payment provider and verify webhook signatures server-side.

Do not assume customers will pay before validating demand.

